The Mercedes-Benz '''M256''' engine is a [[turbocharger|turbocharged]] [[straight-six engine]] produced since 2017, when it was first introduced on the [[Mercedes-Benz S-Class (W222)|W222 S 450]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.motortrend.com/cars/mercedes-benz/s-class/2018/2018-mercedes-benz-s500-european-spec-first-drive-review/|title=2018 Mercedes-Benz S500 European-Spec First Drive: Ice, Ice Baby. Too Cool, Too Cool|website=MotorTrend|date=19 July 2017|access-date=2018-07-03}}</ref> It replaces the previous [[Mercedes-Benz M276 engine|M276 V6 engine]],<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.motor1.com/news/227616/mercedes-phases-out-v6-engines/|title=Mercedes To Phase Out V6 Engines In Favor Of Straight-Sixes|work=Motor1.com|access-date=2018-07-03|language=en}}</ref> and is Mercedes' first petrol-powered straight-six engine since the [[Mercedes-Benz M104 engine|M104 engine]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.marklines.com/en/report_all/rep1752_201808|title=Mercedes-Benz M256 inline 6-cylinder gasoline engine - MarkLines Automotive Industry Portal}}</ref>

== Design ==
The M256 shares a modular design with other [[Straight-four engine|inline-four]] and [[V8 engine]]s, which are all {{convert|500|cc|abbr=on}} per cylinder.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://autoweek.com/article/technology/why-mercedes-new-inline-six-matters-even-if-no-one-sure-when-well-see-it|title=Why Mercedes' new inline six matters, even if no one is sure when we'll see it|work=Autoweek|access-date=2018-07-03}}</ref> It uses an aluminium alloy block with [[overhead camshaft|dual overhead camshafts]] and has [[Multi-valve#Four valves|4 valves]] per cylinder.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.automobilemag.com/news/first-drive-2018-mercedes-benz-s-class/|title=First Drive: 2018 Mercedes-Benz S-Class {{!}} Automobile Magazine|date=2017-07-18|work=Automobile Magazine|access-date=2018-07-03|language=en-US}}</ref> The M256 uses a 48V electrical system to operate a [[BorgWarner]] [[electric supercharger]],<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.wired.com/2017/04/little-fan-fixes-turbochargers-biggest-problem/ |first1=Alex |last1=Davies |title=This Little Fan Could Fix the Turbocharger's Biggest Problem—And Make Cars Way More Efficient|magazine=WIRED|access-date=2018-07-03|language=en-US}}</ref> which can spin up to 70,000 rpm to reduce turbo lag.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.caranddriver.com/news/nine-things-you-must-know-about-the-new-mercedes-inline-six|title=Nine Things You Must Know about the New Mercedes Inline-Six|work=Car and Driver|access-date=2018-07-03|language=en}}</ref> In the S 500's G variant engine, an integrated starter [[alternator (automotive)|alternator]] also provides up to {{convert|16|kW|PS|0|abbr=on}} and {{convert|250|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of boost, and replaces the drive belts by managing the water pump and air conditioning; allowing for a smaller and lighter engine.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/car-technology/news/a31366/mercedes-benz-m256-inline-six/|title=Why Mercedes-Benz Is Going All In on Straight-Sixes|date=2016-11-01|work=Road & Track|access-date=2018-07-03|language=en-US}}</ref>

